# Public authentication evidence · `guide:authentication:public-evidence`

**Section (Authentication claim area)** · `sec:authentication:area-authentication` Incorrect public byte recipes or script interpretation can attach authority to the wrong author. The public recipes and fixed-size carriers implement the public subset of (`[ROOT-dec:design:authentication-owner]`) under (`[ROOT-amend:decomposition:act-authentication]`).

**Definition (Conditional public evidence)** · `def:authentication:public-evidence` AssetId stores display-order asset bytes; consensus encodings reverse them explicitly. KeyHash is an opaque declared digest, not a parsed key. Qualification decodes exactly one canonical PNAT script and leaves output and issuance validation to the wallet. Key-script decoding accepts exactly the canonical PNAK script. These byte recipes neither sign, verify a signature, fetch a chain nor authenticate supplied evidence. Hashing an opaque 32-byte encoding follows (`[ROOT-amend:identity:signing-key-hash]`); invalid point refusal belongs to membership and verification. Initial asset derivation follows (`[ROOT-amend:identity:author-asset-recipe]`) through RustCrypto SHA-256 and its compression primitive, independently of Elements' implementation.

**Record (Public recipe evidence)** · `rec:authentication:evidence` The committed golden values in tests/golden.json contain three version-two Elements regtest daemon-issued, signed and mined transactions with known prevouts including a nonzero output index, contract fields and the daemon's returned asset identifiers. Two also retain reordered input presentations accepted by testmempoolaccept with unchanged ALL-with-ANYONECANPAY witnesses before the originals were broadcast; the alternatives are not separately mined spends. The rows cover a single issuance, issuance with a fee input, and two amount-one issuances with only one marked. Runs are not invariant under byte reversal. The original three version-one transactions remain an unsupported-version refusal corpus. The contract_rpc field is the RPC display spelling; contract is the reversed raw consensus field, checked against the transaction bytes. Regenerate with python3 -B ci/authentication_vectors.py --generate packages/act-authentication/tests/golden.json in the declared Elements service environment. The fixture reader independently recomputes every retained issuance entropy, asset and token identifier through SHA-256 compression, and Elements regtest display transaction identifiers from the raw non-witness serialization. Unused decoded daemon fields are absent; witness signatures, witness programs, supplied funding locators and daemon provenance remain explicitly unrecomputed inputs, not chain evidence. Python hashlib independently supplies the key digests and key scripts; no unit test starts a daemon. (`claim:authentication:asset-identifiers-match-daemon`), (`claim:authentication:key-hashes-and-scripts-match-independent-values`) and (`claim:authentication:qualification-is-one-canonical-script`) check these finite public obligations. They do not establish live token history or close signature dependency admission.

**Definition (Public verification admission)** · `def:authentication:verification-admission` The verification module supplies VerificationKey::parse, VerificationKey::verify and membership_key under (`[ROOT-amend:crypto:authentication-entry]`). A key can be created only by parsing exactly 32 bytes as an x-only point. Verification accepts exactly a 64-byte BIP-340 signature and a 32-byte message digest, with message length checked first and both lengths checked before native work. membership_key parses first, then computes the unchanged key digest; its result is a candidate for comparison, not a token-membership verdict. VerificationError distinguishes KeyLength, InvalidKey, MessageLength, SignatureLength, ContextCapacity and InvalidSignature. It carries no input payload. Public arguments and results are observed in full; no secret exists in this interface. The operations await independent integration review and are not an enabled act verifier.

**Record (Publication validity and wrapper admission)** · `rec:authentication:signature-fixtures` tests/bip340.csv is the complete unchanged nineteen-row BIP-340 publication from bitcoin/bips revision 200f9b26fe0a2f235a2af8b30c4be9f12f6bc9cb, at bip-0340/test-vectors.csv. The publication is available at https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/200f9b26fe0a2f235a2af8b30c4be9f12f6bc9cb/bip-0340/test-vectors.csv. ci/authentication_bip340.py checks its SHA-256, independently computes the tagged challenge with hashlib and the curve equation with Python integers, and agrees with all nineteen published results at their original lengths. (`claim:authentication:published-signatures-have-fixed-message-admission`) verifies valid admitted rows, refuses invalid admitted rows, and gives MessageLength for the four valid messages of 0, 1, 17 and 100 bytes. Nothing pads, hashes or truncates a message to fit. tests/malformed-points.json separates wrong lengths, a point without a curve lift, the field prime and an above-prime encoding; Python independently checks these classes, while the binding's invalid-point causes remain one InvalidKey variant (`claim:authentication:malformed-x-only-points-refuse`). (`claim:authentication:signature-binds-key-and-message`), (`claim:authentication:membership-parses-before-hashing`) and (`claim:authentication:length-admission-precedes-native-verification`) witness association and refusal order. Together with (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:wallet-codec-pins-match-review]`), (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:wallet-codec-provenance-matches-build]`) and (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:authentication-verification-admission-is-reproduced]`), these controls supply the finite evidence of (`[ROOT-verif:crypto:public-verification-fixtures]`). Test-only counters live at the called parsing, hashing and verification boundaries and are thread-local. They add no production observation mechanism.

The operation integration dossier is recorded in (`[ROOT-rec:authentication:verification-dossier]`).

**Definition (Canonical authentication values)** · `def:authentication:encoding` The header and material encode (`[ROOT-def:identity:verified-header-name]`) and (`[ROOT-def:identity:authentication-material]`) through pn-interchange. Header decoding retains the supplied canonical bytes and reconstructs original ProposalBody parts, never current removal states. The implemented closed header version is exactly [1,0,0]; the separate version-two header under (`def:authentication:version-two`) is not accepted by this version-one decoder. Canonical decoding refuses indefinite lengths, nonpreferred heads, reordered or duplicate keys and trailing bytes. Recipe identifiers, fixed byte lengths and editions are checked separately. Representation input is limited to 2,097,152 bytes before decoding, with the proposal profile’s independent limits retained; (`[ROOT-def:identity:verified-header-name]`) derives that ceiling from the profile body bound and fixed header overhead. Comparison uses independently supplied original parts and retained disclosure, not a recomputed private proposal or signature verdict.

**Definition (Role-specific authentication messages)** · `def:authentication:messages` ProposalMessage retains P only from pn-act-profile’s private-field ComputedProposalCommitment. SealMessage and ApprovalMessage use their distinct admitted frames under (`[ROOT-amend:identity:host-seal]`) and (`[ROOT-amend:identity:approval-signature]`). `unopened_proposal_digest` returns a distinct UnestablishedProposalDigest: a signature over it establishes only a signature over supplied bytes, not the proposal relation, which requires recomputation from openings. Private tuple fields distinguish constructed roles but do not establish provenance for supplied digests. Distinct framed preimages provide domain separation when P is profile-computed; unequal digests across all possible inputs additionally depend on the admitted collision assumption, not a finite fixture test. The standalone retirement message and marker wrapper are absent under (`[ROOT-rule:identity:retirement-order]`); the designed per-act field is not implemented by these version-one operations.

**Record (Authentication encoding evidence)** · `rec:authentication:encoding-evidence` (`[ROOT-verif:identity:authentication-encoding]`) is witnessed by (`claim:authentication:canonical-header-material`), (`claim:authentication:role-messages-match-golden-values`), (`claim:authentication:header-comparison-refuses-each-field`) and (`[ROOT-claim:discipline:authentication-encoding-values-independent]`). The independent Python generator uses standard-library SHA-256 and its own canonical array and map framing, reusing ci/storage_vectors.py for primitive values. The Rust tests read committed golden bytes without invoking a generator. These finite checks establish encoding and comparison conformance, not signing, key membership or authenticated ordering. The public B projection and inverse are owned by pn-act-profile and called directly. Every golden header’s decoded parts and retained openings recompute P through its public commitment operation. These claims retain their actual execution outcomes and witness only the preceding one-nonce, version-one reference construction in the exact fixture scope described here. They do not establish network-v0.32.0 implementation; the enclosing new-tag obligation remains held on (`[ROOT-open:identity:act-realization-admission]`). realizes{``[ROOT-open:identity:act-realization-admission]``; This scopes legacy evidence and retains the new-tag implementation hold, without proving private entropy.} The separate version-two evidence is (`claim:authentication:version-two-golden-values`), (`claim:authentication:version-two-refusals`), (`claim:authentication:version-two-dependency-proof`) and (`claim:authentication:version-two-header-comparison-refuses-each-field`) and (`claim:authentication:version-two-header-domain-refusals`), scoped by (`def:authentication:version-two`); all remaining authority and carriage holds persist.

**Definition (Version-two header and messages)** · `def:authentication:version-two` The separate v2 surface implements the closed major-two header and material of (`[ROOT-def:identity:verified-header-name]`) and (`[ROOT-def:identity:authentication-material]`), retaining exact canonical bytes and comparing all original fields with the supplied disclosure. It refuses missing retirement data and foreign editions without conversion. Proposal messages contain computed P; seal and approval use their unchanged v1 roles over the new header bytes. No operation signs, verifies a signature, establishes membership or enables service carriage. Existing v1 APIs and bytes remain unchanged. Conformance evidence (`claim:authentication:version-two-golden-values`) uses three independent fixtures in unsorted case order 3,0,7; (`claim:authentication:version-two-dependency-proof`) checks the three-part proof with zero, two and 1024 dependencies; (`claim:authentication:version-two-refusals`) checks representation and identity refusals. The robustness controls (`claim:authentication:version-two-header-comparison-refuses-each-field`) and (`claim:authentication:version-two-header-domain-refusals`) change each compared field and the namespace and three recipe keys separately, asserting the named refusals. Rust tests read the committed corpus; the gate hash audit independently regenerates it through the authentication-version-two-fixtures family and compares every value. Declaring an interpreter prerequisite for the Rust suite would duplicate that gate check and is not selected. The independent producer uses its own canonical framing and standard-library SHA-256, calling no production code or helper. The corpus material actor key differs from the key named by its author identifier; it is an encoding fixture, not an author-binding fixture. These finite executions establish neither that a content opening exists nor that disclosed material binds to the act author: an unopened commitment binds to at most one content. They do not discharge private entropy, custody, public authority or the later retained-carriage obligations.

**Record (Dependency-opening evidence across editions)** · `rec:authentication:dependency-opening-evidence` The finite evidence for (`[ROOT-verif:design:dependency-opening-cases]`) keeps the editions separate. (`[ACTPROFILE-claim:profile:opened-replay-preserves-structure-and-title]`), (`[ACTPROFILE-claim:profile:fixed-recipes-bind-exact-authored-inputs]`), (`[ACTPROFILE-claim:profile:protected-inputs-retain-fixed-commitments]`) and (`[ACTPROFILE-claim:profile:proposal-vectors-cover-projection-branches]`) retain their version-one fixture scope, including supplied anchors and the explicitly conditional verifier. (`[ROOT-claim:hashcitations:act-profile-vector-corruptions-refuse]`) checks the independent version-one corpus against specified corruptions. The separate version-two evidence is (`claim:authentication:version-two-golden-values`) and (`claim:authentication:version-two-dependency-proof`): three independently generated cases compare the admitted recipes and the three-part opening against supplied P,C_D, with changed opening parts refusing. Neither edition's finite evidence establishes authentic anchors, finalized placement, signature integration, production entropy, custody or retained version-two carriage.
